  "conversion_warnings": [
    "Source: Syracuse University 2025-2026 CourseLeaf academic catalog program page (https://coursecatalog.syracuse.edu/undergraduate/engineering-computer-science/computer-science-bs/). The course-search app at coursecatalog.syracuse.edu/course-search/ is a JS SPA; course descriptions, credits, and prerequisites were instead pulled from the static CourseLeaf course-list pages (.../undergraduate/courses/cis/, /cse/, /mat/, /phy/, /che/, /bio/, /phi/, /crs/, /ist/, /wrt/, /ecs/), which expose courseblock prerequisite text.",
    "Syracuse course numbers are already real 3-digit catalog numbers; no Banner 7-digit de-mangling was needed.",
    "total_credits = 120 (stated graduation minimum). Credit reconciliation: intro_major 6 (ECS101 3 + CIS151 3) + cs_core 34 + upper_div_technical_electives 18 + mathematics 15 + natural_sciences 8 + writing 6 + presentation_skills 3 + ssh_required 6 (PHI251 3 + ECS392 3) + ssh_electives 15 + first_year_seminar 1 + free_electives 8 = 120. The representative program of study lists 118-121 due to MAT296 variable credit (2-4) and the MAT397(4)/MAT331(3) choice; modeled MAT296 at 4 and the math section at the stated 'at least 15 credits'.",
    "Catalog wording: 'The major section consists of ECS 101, CIS 151, the 34-credit computer science core, and 18 credits of upper-division technical electives.' Modeled ECS101+CIS151 as intro_major (all), the 11-course core as cs_core (all, 34 cr), and the electives as upper_div_technical_electives (select, 18 cr).",
    "CS Core is 34 credits across 11 courses: CIS252 (4) + CIS341/351/352 (3 each) + CSE384 (3) + CIS375 (3) + CIS453/454/473/477 (3 each) + CSE486 (3) = 4 + 30 = 34. Verified against the catalog Computer Science Core table.",
    "Upper Division Technical Electives (18 cr): catalog allows (a) any CIS or CSE course numbered above 300 (unless excluded), (b) any MAT course numbered above 400 except specifically-excluded MAT 521 (Introduction to Probability) and MAT 485 (Differential Equations and Matrix Algebra for Engineers), and (c) the listed philosophy courses PHI 378, 451, 551, 552. Modeled as a select pool: from.include ['CIS:300+','CSE:300+','MAT:400+'] + from.courses [PHI378,PHI451,PHI551,PHI552], excluding the core/required CIS/CSE courses already counted plus MAT485 and MAT521. The catalog rule 'at least 9 of the 18 credits must be in CIS/CSE' is a sub-constraint the schema cannot fully express; documented here and noted in the requirement name.",
    "Mathematics Section (>=15 cr): MAT295 Calculus I (4), MAT296 Calculus II (2-4, modeled at 4), MAT397 Calculus III (4) OR MAT331 First Course in Linear Algebra (3) modeled as choice '{MAT397, MAT331}', and CIS321 Introduction to Probability and Statistics (4). CIS321 is the program's required statistics course and is placed in the mathematics section per the catalog; it is excluded from the CIS technical-elective pool to prevent double counting.",
    "Natural Sciences (8 cr): per the program page, PHY211 (3) + PHY221 lab (1) are UNCONDITIONALLY required ('PHY 211 ... PHY 221 ...'), then 'In addition, students must complete one of the following options' = one of three 4-credit lecture+lab pairs: {[PHY212,PHY222], [CHE106,CHE107], [BIO121,BIO122]}. Modeled as a standalone required requirement (natural_sciences_physics_core, type:all [PHY211,PHY221]) plus a one_of (natural_sciences_second_sequence) over the three lecture+lab bundles, so the physics-core pair reads as required rather than conditional. Coreqs set bidirectionally for each lecture/lab pair.",
    "General Education (47 cr total): 6 cr writing (WRT105 + WRT205), 3 cr presentational skills (one of CRS225/CRS325/IST344), 8 cr natural sciences (modeled under supporting), 21 cr social science & humanities = PHI251 + ECS392 (6 cr required) + 15 cr A/SS/H electives, plus the 1 cr First Year Seminar and 8 cr free electives. The 15 cr SS/H electives, 1 cr FYS, and 8 cr free electives are modeled as external wildcard '*:*' pools because the catalog draws them from broad Arts & Sciences / VPA offerings not enumerated in this CS-scoped course set.",
    "Academic standards (not modeled as hard constraints): no grade below C- satisfies the 6 cr writing, the mathematics section, the CS core, or the 18 cr upper-division technical electives; the CS core must be completed with a B- (2.667) average.",
    "Prerequisites deep-traced from the CourseLeaf course-list pages (cross-validated against the live catalog 2026-06). Real CSE OR-alternatives are PRESERVED exactly as the catalog states them and the alternative courses are defined so the refs resolve: CIS351 'CIS 252 or CSE 283' -> CIS252|CSE283; CIS453 'CIS 351 or CSE 382' -> CIS351|CSE382; CSE384 'CSE 283 or CIS 351' -> CSE283|CIS351; CSE486 '(CIS 341 or CSE 381) and (CSE 384 and CIS 351)' -> (CIS341|CSE381)&CSE384&CIS351. MAT296 'MAT 295 with minimum grade C-' -> MAT295[C-]; MAT397 'MAT 296 with minimum grade C-' -> MAT296[C-]. Where a prerequisite OR-branch referenced a course outside the modeled corpus (e.g. CPS196 alt to CIS151 for CIS252, MAT375 alt to CIS375 for CIS473, MAT534/541 alts for CIS428), the expression was reduced to the defined in-corpus branch to avoid dangling references; those alternative paths are equivalent for plan generation. CSE prerequisite-chain courses (CSE283, CSE382, CSE381, CSE261) are defined so the CIS/CSE OR-branches resolve.",
    "cip_code 11.0101 (Computer and Information Sciences, General) per the build spec; system_type semester."
